Plus. Why we use CLI?
"There are several reasons to learn how to use the CLI:"

(1): Most bioinformatics tools have no graphical interfaces. they use the CLI/ shell.

(2): CLI allows you to work more efficiently. Repetitive tasks automated. Tedious tasks simplified.

(3): To use remote computers or cloud computing


1. Introducing the Shell

Abridgements
    How to access the Shell? -> Terminal (Mac)
    Bash(Terminal)
        pwd : print working directory
        ls : listing files
            ls -F : listing files with type such as /(directory), *(executable), @(symbolic link)
        cd + (name) : change directory
        man : manual, detailed documentation.
            "Space" to next page,
            "b" to go backwards
            "q" to quit
        "tab" : automated filling, more press towards to other possible choices
        "up arrow" : select previous command

    Key Points
        Useful commands for navigating your file system include: ls, pwd, and cd.
        Most commands take options (flags) which begin with a -.
        Tab completion can reduce errors from mistyping and make work more efficient in the shell.


2. Navigating Files and Directories

Abridgements
    Flags can be combined together. ex. "ls -Fa"
    You can list the contents of any directory by passing its path to ls without changing your current location.
    Using absolute or relative paths with cd allows you to jump directly to any target folder in a single command.
    Full Path always starts with /. Relatives doesn't.
    . : working directory
    .. : parental directory
    / : highest class (root directory)
    ~ : home directory

Key Points
    The /, ~, and .. characters represent important navigational shortcuts.
    Hidden files and directories start with . and can be viewed using ls -a.
    Relative paths specify a location starting from the current location, while absolute paths specify a location from the root of the file system.


